{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Now Korah son of Izhar son of Kohath son of Levi took.</b> Many interpretations attend this act of 'taking,' since Scripture did not specify whether the taking was with the hands or with words, as in ״קַח אֶת הַלְוִיִּם״ (Numbers 8:6). Some say that the entire narrative extends as far as the children of Reuben — that he took for himself, for his faction, the children of Reuben, for he sought to gather to himself every embittered soul, and he knew that their souls were bitter over the birthright that had been taken from Reuben. And even though it was Jacob who took it and not Moses, in any case — since all authority would revert to its owners, they would reckon that the birthright would also revert to them, and they would join Korah. Some say that he took for his argument 'son of Izhar,' for this was the main thrust of his claim, saying: my father's brothers were four, etc. — since I am a son of Izhar, who is fit to take the second rank, as Rashi explains. Some say that the ו of וְדָתָן is superfluous, like the ו of ״וְאַיָּה וַעֲנָה״ (Genesis 36:24), and its meaning is that he took Dathan and Abiram to join them to himself. And it is sound to say that both are the case: that he took for his argument the fact that he was a son of Izhar, and also took Dathan and Abiram and On, and according to this the ו would not be superfluous." "And in the Midrash (Bemidbar Rabbah 18:3) they said: 'Now Korah took' — what did he take? A garment that was entirely tekhelet, etc. And it concludes by asking further: a house full of books — is it obligated in a mezuzah? etc. Granted regarding tzitzit he derived it from proximity — since 'Now Korah took' is adjacent to the passage of tzitzit; but mezuzah — who mentioned its name? And it appears to me that he derives it from what is written: ״וְדָתָן וַאֲבִירָם יָצְאוּ נִצָּבִים פֶּתַח אָהֳלֵיהֶם״ — why does it need to write ״פֶּתַח אָהֳלֵיהֶם״? Rather, they went out and stood to blaspheme and to revile the mezuzah that was at the entrance of their tents. And so Korah disputed the passage of tzitzit, and Dathan and Abiram further added their dispute over the commandment of the mezuzah at the entrance of the tent, for they said that Moses invented these two commandments from his own heart. And this was of a piece with their factional dispute, as our master Rabbenu Bachye wrote, for they said: just as a garment that is entirely tekhelet — logic dictates that it is exempt from tzitzit, and likewise a house full of books is exempt from mezuzah — so too when the entire congregation is holy they have no need for a leader. And further hinted at in this is that the essential reason for these two commandments is to serve a person as reminders of the commandments of HaShem, as it is written ״וּרְאִיתֶם אוֹתוֹ וּזְכַרְתֶּם״ etc., and likewise the mezuzah, so that one remember HaShem when coming in and going out — but when the entire congregation is holy they have no need for a reminder, for even without this HaShem is before them always — He, blessed be He, and all His commandments, and they are needed only for people who forget Elohim. And for this reason they themselves and their little ones were punished. And one must ponder: if Korah and his assembly sinned, why do their little ones come into this punishment? But it is because they disputed tzitzit and mezuzah, and the Gemara concludes in the chapter Bameh Madlikin (Shabbat 32b): Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Meir — one said: children die on account of the sin of tzitzit, and one said: on account of mezuzah, etc. And the reason for both is one, for both come for remembrance — the mezuzah so that HaShem be before one's eyes always when coming in and going out, and tzitzit for remembrance of the commandments — and it is written (Hosea 4:6): ״וַתִּשְׁכַּח תּוֹרַת אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֶשְׁכַּח בָּנֶיךָ גַּם אָנִי״, for when you forget the offspring of good deeds, I will forget even your ordinary offspring. And further, the children are a branch of him, like tzitzit, which are a branch of the garment." "<b>Now Korah son of Izhar, etc.</b> And he did not count him as 'son of Jacob,' because Korah was flattering the children of Reuben and said, in the way of flattery: I do not trace my lineage after our father Jacob, and let my portion not be with him in that he took the birthright from Reuben — therefore the verse also does not trace his lineage after him. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 109b): ״וַיִּקַּח קֹרַח״ — that he made a bald spot in the world. ״בֶּן יִצְהָר״ — that he heated the entire world like midday, etc. ״בֶּן לֵוִי״ — that he became an accompaniment to Gehenna. And why not also count ״בֶּן יַעֲקֹב,״ since he pressed himself into Gehenna? Jacob begged for mercy on his own behalf, that he not be counted with them, as it is said (Genesis 49:5): ״בְּסֹדָם אַל תָּבֹא נַפְשִׁי בִּקְהָלָם אַל תֵּחַד כְּבֹדִי.״ According to its plain sense, it would certainly not be to his honor if he were counted with them, for through this his name would be expounded to his disgrace, and therefore he asked that his honor not be joined there. Another thing: lest they say that this trait was an inheritance in his hand from Jacob, who contested the birthright — that is, the priesthood — for in former times the service was performed by the firstborn, and they would say that from him Korah learned to contest the priesthood. And likewise in the matter of Zimri, they would say that it was an inheritance in their hands from Jacob, who took two sisters. Therefore he traced the lineage of Korah the zealot only as far as Levi, who was also zealous against Joseph. And yet the statement still requires explanation: what was the bald spot he made, and how did he heat the world like midday?" "And according to the hint from the verse aside from the statement of the Gemara, there would be room to explain the names in another way. And that is, that Scripture certainly does not depart from its plain sense — the names in their plain meaning — but since it specified this entire lineage, it appears that his name and the names of his ancestors pointed to what would come: that they would produce one who breaches the fence of the world, namely Korah, for the names hint at his arguments and at his dispute. For קֹרַח means a baldness of hair, and as the Gemara concludes in the chapter Ḥelek (Sanhedrin 110b): 'Go and assay it, and comb it out,' etc. — he makes sport of you for having made you bald of the hairs. And so it turns out that he took for himself as an argument against Moses the fact that he made the Levites bald. ״בֶּן יִצְהָר״ — this is an expression of oil, that he disputed the priesthood, because he poured anointing oil on Aaron's head, the half of which is two hundred and fifty, as is found in the portion Ki Tissa (Exodus 30:23), and therefore he took for his faction two hundred and fifty men. Although some say he took this number so that it would come out for every tribe as the number of a minor Sanhedrin — when you add to them three more: Dathan and Abiram and On, apart from the tribe of Levi from which there was no man among them — in any case according to our approach we may say that he took this number for this reason: to say, these are the sons of Izhar who are worthy of being anointed with the anointing oil, the half of which is two hundred and fifty, just as Aaron was anointed with it. ״בֶּן קְהָת״ — this is from the expression ״וְלוֹ יִקְּהַת עַמִּים״ (Genesis 49:10), which means gathering and assembly, for he gathered them and his spirit assembled them to rally them against Moses and Aaron, as it is written: ״וַיִּקָּהֲלוּ עַל מֹשֶׁה וְאַהֲרֹן.״ ״בֶּן לֵוִי״ — Rashi explained in the portion Vayetze (Genesis 29:34) that he was called Levi on account of being accompanied with gifts, and the gifts too Korah took for himself as an argument against Aaron, as the Yalkut concludes on this portion (section remez 750): the matter of a certain widow who had one lamb — she came to shear it, Aaron came and took the first of the fleece, etc. Regarding all these, the expression 'taking' applies, for all of them he took for himself as arguments to reinforce his dispute." "However, according to what the Midrash concludes — ״קֹרַח,״ that he made a bald spot in the world, etc. — it appears that the intent of the author of the Midrash is to say that Korah disputed the very existence of the Name, blessed be He, as it is written: ״הַנּוֹעָדִים עַל ה׳״ (Numbers 27:3), ״בְּהַצֹּתָם עַל ה׳״ (Numbers 26:9) — and where do we find that Korah conspired against HaShem? For even though our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 110a) that one who disputes his master is as one who disputes the Shekhinah, in any case this itself is a difficulty: why should he be considered as one who disputes the Shekhinah? Rather, it is because Korah claimed that the world is primordial, co-eternal with the primordiality of the Name, blessed be He, and that it is necessary in its existence — and he needed this denial in order to strengthen his dispute, for he claimed and said: ״כִּי כָל הָעֵדָה כֻּלָּם קְדֹשִׁים וּבְתוֹכָם ה׳ וּמַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ עַל קְהַל ה׳,״ and his intent was to say that there is no superiority or dominion of any created being over its fellow, just as there is no priority, God forbid, to Him, blessed be He, over His creatures. And this is what he said: ״וּבְתוֹכָם ה׳״ — and it would have been fitting for HaShem to be at their head, not 'in their midst' in the middle; rather, he intimated and acknowledged, as it were, that there is no priority to Him — all the more so, by an a fortiori argument from the son of an a fortiori argument, why do you exalt yourselves? And Moses said to him: ״בֹּקֶר״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, set boundaries in His world; can you turn morning into evening? So too can you abolish this — as it is said (Genesis 1:4–5): ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹּקֶר,״ ״וַיַּבְדֵּל,״ and it is written: ״וָאַבְדִּיל אֶתְכֶם מִן הָעַמִּים לִהְיוֹת לִי״ (Leviticus 20:26), and just as He separated Israel from the nations, so He separated Aaron, etc. (Rashi in the name of the Midrash)." "And every discerning person will be astounded at the vision — what is the relevance of the vision of evening and morning here? But it is because Korah disputed the very root of the law, saying that the world is primordial, and the appearance of evening and morning is all mere chance, and if so, why should such a thing proceed from before Him, blessed be He, to distinguish between one man and his fellow. And Moses answered him from that very thing as a proof, for chance does not persist for a long time, and behold, our eyes see that you cannot abolish the distinction of morning and evening; if so, this is not chance but thus was it created by His will. And just as He, blessed be He, is primordial to all that was formed, so He apportioned of His honor to whom He desired in it, and assigned boundaries — to the day He gave its boundary by day, and Israel He set above all the nations; so too He gave, by His will, blessed be He, this greatness to Aaron. And this is what it says: ״אִם בְּרִיאָה יִבְרָא ה׳״ and so forth. Rashi explained: 'אִם בְּרִיאָה' — if a mouth for the earth was created during the six days of Creation, מוּטָב; and if not — 'יִבְרָא ה׳'. And this is difficult for me: why was 'כִּי טוֹב' not said of it, for we do not find the expression מוּטָב explicitly in the verse. Rather, surely this is its meaning: Korah disputed the Creation of the six days of Genesis and claimed that these things never were, and I say that there was a Creation; therefore I say ״אִם בְּרִיאָה״ — that is, if there was a new Creation in the six days of Genesis, then ״יִבְרָא ה׳״ a new thing in the earth ״וּפָצְתָה אֶת פִּיהָ״ and so forth, ״וְיָדְעוּ כִּי נִאֲצוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים הָאֵלֶּה אֶת ה׳״ in that they said there was never a Creation — and now they will believe retroactively that there was a Creation, just as HaShem created a new thing in the earth now." "And this is the opinion of Rava (in Nedarim 39b) who expounded the verse ״שֶׁמֶשׁ יָרֵחַ עָמַד זְבֻלָה״ (Habakkuk 3:11) — what did they seek in Zevul? Rather, in the dispute of Korah they ascended to Zevul, and so forth. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'For My honor you shall not be annihilated,' and so forth. And that is the reason, because Korah railed against the boundaries of morning and evening, which are sun and moon, and he claimed concerning them that it was not HaShem who apportioned to each one its boundary and its domain — this one by day and this one by night; therefore they ascended from the firmament to Zevul, for it is not fitting for them to give light to one who does not believe that it was not HaShem who wrought all this. But it was not seemly that they should say, 'Let judgment be done upon the son of Amram,' until the Holy One, blessed be He, said to them: 'For My honor you shall not be annihilated,' and so forth — and behold, the matter breaches and rises to the Throne of Glory and touches upon My honor more than upon the honor of Moses. And why they ascended specifically to Zevul — I would explain this because Korah railed against the priesthood, and they said in the Midrash concerning the tamid offering of morning and evening that by their merit the luminaries of sun and moon, the lights of day and night, endure; and if the priesthood were abolished and fell into the hand of one unworthy of it, then by what merit would the luminaries endure? Therefore they ascended to Zevul, for in it is the Temple and Michael the high priest, as is stated in tractate Ḥagigah (12b). And from here there is a proof that Korah disputed the boundaries of sun and moon and the Creation itself; therefore they said that he made a bald spot in the world, for when His divinity, blessed be He, is removed, as it were, the entire world becomes a קָרְחָה. And in that the sun and moon ascended to Zevul, the sun had already emerged from its sheath, and through this it heated the entire world like midday. And one who touches upon the matter of divinity causes the blunting of the teeth of those who believe in Him, blessed be He, as it is written concerning Egypt that their teeth were blunted when they saw the binding of the ram (Tur O.C. §430) — therefore they said ״בֶּן קְהָת״ — that he blunted the teeth of his forebears. And let this suffice for what we have said by way of a degree of derash, and now let us come to the explanation of the dispute according to its plain sense."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {4 ["God"]}}, :notes {6 "The phrase מִטּוּנָךְ in comment [0] appears to be a textual variant or corruption; I have rendered it as 'from that very thing' based on the flow of the argument (i.e., Moses' proof comes from the very phenomenon Korah cited), though the reading is uncertain. Similarly, מוּטָב is presented as Rashi's paraphrase of כִּי טוֹב, which the Kli Yakar then questions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:22.558025259Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}